  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 130 PM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nA 593 to 594 dam upper high at 500 mb will be centered over the OK \nand TX panhandles with the western portion nosing in eastern NM \nSunday. Diurnal showers and thunderstorms will once again get going \nacross the western and central high terrain midday with an overall \nstorm motion north-northwest south of I-40 and north-northeast along \nand north of I-40. Storm motion across the Sacramento Mountains \nSunday afternoon should help direct storms west and away from the \nRuidoso burn scars. Once again, any storm will be capable of \nproducing locally heavy rainfall and flash flooding in low lying, \npoor drainage, and urban areas.\n\nThe upper low over the Pacific NW finally moves inland to over the \nnorthern Rockies Monday shifting the upper high east to over \nOklahoma, Missouri and western Arkansas. The position of these two \nupper level features will help position the monsoon plume from \nsouthwest to northeast NM with an overall storm motion to the \nnortheast at 10 to 20 mph. The upper low exits northeast to the \nDakotas and central Canada Tuesday flattening the upper high from \neastern NM to the mid Atlantic and strengthening to 597 dam over the \nmid Mississippi River Valley. The monsoon plume and thus better \nshower and storm coverage will remain across western and central NM \ndue to this part of the state remaining on the western flank of the \nupper high circulation. The upper high looks to shift back to over \nNew Mexico as early as Wednesday. This will help disrupt the monsoon \nplume with recycled moisture helping to keep diurnal shower and \nstorm chances across the higher terrain. PWATS looks to decrease \nfrom 0.9 to 1.3 inches Monday to 0.8 to 1 inch late next week due to \nthe upper high moving overhead. Highs next week will be in the upper \n80s to mid 90s across lower elevations of western and central NM and \nmid to upper 90s across eastern NM which is slightly above average \nfor early to mid September",
